   "rationale": "This is the eye that lets me see who is at the public door.\n\nIt takes the decoded public payload, extracts `msg` and `sig` (or `signature`) from the top-level JSON object, and asks ssh-keygen to verify that the signature covers exactly those message bytes, in namespace `mshkn`, under a key in a baked-in allowed_signers file. On success it prints the principal name from that file and exits 0; on any failure it prints nothing and exits 1. Fail-closed everywhere: unparseable payload, plain text, missing/empty sig, unknown key, wrong namespace, tampered message.\n\nallowed_signers contains exactly one line \u2014 the key you gave me, with `namespaces=\"mshkn\"` so a signature you made for some other purpose cannot be replayed at my door:\n  mike namespaces=\"mshkn\" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIBheydtYRNpBtxjv3LMaW7UR4C9PdBdlXoLeJRxzqtNW\nThe key is public; nothing secret is in the image, and I never hold your private key.\n\nNotes on the implementation. The base image has no jq and no python3, so the JSON is parsed by a small awk parser (/verb/json.awk) that walks the top-level object properly rather than pattern-matching: a string containing `\"sig\":\"bogus\"` or a stray `}` cannot confuse it. Because `ssh-keygen -Y sign` signs a file's exact bytes and it is ambiguous whether your shell appended a trailing newline, I try three byte-variants of the message: exact, plus-newline, and trailing-newline-stripped. The principal name is checked against [A-Za-z0-9_.-] before being printed, so nothing strange can be injected into my principal's name.\n\nTested in trials t-2 and t-3 (mawk 1.3.4, OpenSSH on Ubuntu 24.04) \u2014 pass: valid signature -> `mike`; newline mismatch in either direction; pretty-printed JSON; extra fields in any order; embedded decoy `\"sig\"`; UTF-8 and \\u escapes; 100 KB message (<1s); payload via argv or stdin. Fail closed: wrong namespace, tampered message, unknown key, empty sig, plain text, truncated JSON, `{}`, `[1,2,3]`, empty payload.\n\nTwo things I could not test and one limitation you should weigh:\n1. I don't know how the membrane hands the payload to a hook, so the script accepts it either as argv[1] or on stdin (with a 5s timeout so it can never hang).\n2. I assume `asserts: \"ssh\"` plus stdout `mike` yields a principal named `ssh:mike`. If it is spelled differently, tell me and I will match the policy to it.\n3. No replay protection. This verb is ephemeral, so it cannot remember a signature it has already seen; anyone who observes one of your signed messages can resend those exact bytes and be seen as you. If that matters, approve this first, then ask me for v2: a `chain`-state version keeping a ledger of seen signature digests. That costs you nothing but a habit \u2014 vary your message, or include a timestamp/nonce beside it.",
